+
+## Private 1.0 planning statistics
+
+The native writer stores approximate distinct counts with each table's counts and widths.
+It merges the column sketches already built while writing pages. Opening a database reads the
+table statistics; it does not read every group root to recover these estimates.
+
+Encoded estimates stay charged while a published state or older reader retains them. Writers
+create a new generation before changing an estimate. Optional read or copy credit can be denied
+without waiting: the estimate then remains unknown. An unsupported hash domain, incompatible
+numeric scale or missing group also leaves coverage unknown.
+
+These are planning estimates. They do not establish pruning, uniqueness, overflow safety or
+memory admission. SQL DISTINCT and COUNT(DISTINCT) compute exact results. These paths belong to
+the private 1.0 engine; released 0.x storage is unchanged.
